  "output": "Based on this frontend codebase (and consistent with prior analysis already recorded for this repo), here are concrete findings:\n\n## 1. Caching opportunities\n\n**`src/utils/cache.ts`** already has a localStorage cache layer, but it's not used everywhere API calls happen. Views like transcripts/campaigns re-fetch on every mount with no client cache and no TTL/staleness check — `cacheGet`/`cacheSet` exist but nothing enforces expiry:\n\n```ts\n// utils/cache.ts — add a TTL wrapper so stale data doesn't linger forever\nexport function cacheGetWithTTL<T>(key: string, fallback: T, maxAgeMs: number): T {\n  try {\n    const raw = window.localStorage.getItem(`${CACHE_PREFIX}${key}`);\n    if (!raw) return fallback;\n    const { value, ts } = JSON.parse(raw) as { value: T; ts: number };\n    if (Date.now() - ts > maxAgeMs) return fallback;\n    return value;\n  } catch {\n    return fallback;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**`src/api.ts`**'s `request()` has no dedup/in-flight cache — rapid re-renders (e.g. StrictMode double-effects) can double-fire identical GETs. A simple in-flight map keyed by URL would cut duplicate network calls.\n\n## 2. Redundant computations\n\n**`src/utils/transcript.ts` `buildConversationItems`** runs `events.filter(...)`, `.map(...)`, and a `.sort(...)` on every call — if this is called on every render (not memoized) inside a transcript detail view, it's O(n log n) recomputed needlessly. Wrap call sites with `useMemo`:\n\n```ts\nconst items = useMemo(\n  () => buildConversationItems(transcript, events),\n  [transcript, events]\n);\n```\n\n**`src/constants/outcomes.ts`**'s `OUTCOME_CATEGORIES`/`DEFAULT_OUTCOME_RULES` are module-level constants — good — but if any view does `Object.values(OUTCOME_CATEGORIES).filter(...)` per render to build a dropdown, that filtered list should be hoisted/memoized once.\n\n## 3. Unnecessary React re-renders\n\nThis matches the earlier finding **\"Unfiltered Data Re-renders in TranscriptsView Without Component Memoization\"** (obs 1389) — list rows aren't wrapped in `React.memo`, so filtering/sorting the parent list re-renders every row even when a row's own data hasn't changed:\n\n```tsx\nconst TranscriptRow = React.memo(function TranscriptRow({ transcript }: { transcript: Transcript }) {\n  // ...\n});\n```\n\nAlso **\"Campaign Strategy Objects Rebuilt on Every Render Without Memoization\"** (obs 1395) — `dialing_strategy` object literals built inline in JSX props cause child re-renders via referential inequality; wrap construction in `useMemo`.\n\n**`src/App.tsx`** (1173 lines, obs 1390) is a monolith holding most app state — any state update (e.g. a toast) re-renders the whole tree unless children are memoized/split. Splitting view-specific state into the view components themselves would shrink render scope significantly.\n\n## 4. Memory leaks\n\n**`useCountUp.ts`**: `requestAnimationFrame(tick)` has no cancellation on unmount — if `target` changes rapidly (e.g. user navigates away mid-animation), stale RAF callbacks keep calling `setValue` on an unmounted component:\n\n```ts\nuseEffect(() => {\n  if (target === 0) { setValue(0); return; }\n  const start = performance.now();\n  let rafId: number;\n  ref.current?.classList.add('counting');\n  const tick = (now: number) => {\n    const progress = Math.min((now - start) / duration, 1);\n    const eased = 1 - Math.pow(1 - progress, 3);\n    setValue(Math.round(target * eased));\n    if (progress < 1) rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n    else ref.current?.classList.remove('counting');\n  };\n  rafId = requestAnimationFrame(tick);\n  return () => cancelAnimationFrame(rafId); // missing today\n}, [target, duration]);\n```\n\n**`useToasts.ts`**: `setTimeout(() => dismissToast(id), durationMs)` is never cleared on unmount — if the component unmounts before the timer fires, `dismissToast` still runs against a stale closure (harmless here since it's just state, but if `showToast` triggers from a component that unmounts, the timer handle leaks until it fires).\n\n**LiveKit `Room` usage** (per `App.routing.test.tsx` mocks: `.on`, `.connect`, `.disconnect`) — verify every view that creates a `Room` calls `.disconnect()` and removes listeners in a cleanup function; this is the most common WebRTC-related leak pattern (dangling audio tracks/connections after navigating away from the test-call view).\n\n## 5. N+1 patterns\n\nFrontend-side, the closest analogue is **sequential `await` chains in data-loading effects** — already flagged as **\"Multiple Data-Loading Effects in App.tsx Fire Sequentially on Auth\"** (obs 1393). If `campaigns()`, `transcripts()`, `phrases()`, `outcomes()`, `languageSettings()` etc. are awaited one after another instead of via `Promise.all`, login-time load is serialized unnecessarily:\n\n```ts\nconst [campaigns, transcripts, phrases, outcomes, languages] = await Promise.all([\n  api.campaigns(), api.transcripts(), api.phrases(), api.outcomes(), api.languageSettings(),\n]);\n```\n\nTrue N+1 (per-row queries) lives server-side in `callback_worker` (obs 1380–1383, 1391) and analytics aggregation (obs 1392) — already documented, not in this frontend slice.\n\n---\n**Priority**: (1) parallelize App.tsx auth-load effects, (2) memoize `buildConversationItems` call sites + campaign strategy objects, (3) fix `useCountUp`/`useToasts` cleanup, (4) add `React.memo` to list rows in TranscriptsView/CampaignsView.\n",
